6	Wall of text, sorry, but I also had to document some stuff for myself:
7	
8	There are three ways to communicate data using HTTP:
9	- the HTTP verb: for our purposes, PUT and GET,
10	- the HTTP path: '/ping', '/request' etc.,
11	- the HTTP URL parameter(s), e.g. 'method=libNew&dictionary_id=1234'
12	
13	The bridge will use different handlers for communication with the
14	external dictionary library and for communication with the catboost
15	library. Handlers are created based on a combination of the HTTP verb
16	and the HTTP method. More specifically, there will be combinations
17	- GET + '/extdict_ping'
18	- PUT + '/extdict_request'
19	- GET + '/catboost_ping'
20	- PUT + '/catboost_request'.
21	For each combination, the bridge expects a certain set of URL
22	parameters, e.g. for the first combination parameter "dictionary_id" is
23	expected.
24	
25	Starting with this commit, the library-bridge creates handlers based on
26	the first two combinations (the latter two combinations will be added
27	later). This makes the handler creation mechanism consistent with it's
28	counterpart in xdbc-bridge.
29	
30	For that, it was necessary to make both IBridgeHelper methods
31	"getMainURI()" and "getPingURI()" pure virtual so that derived classes
32	(LibraryBridgeHelper and XDBCBridgeHelper) must provide custom URLs with
33	custom paths.
34	
35	Side note 1: Previously, LibraryBridgeHelper sent HTTP URL parameter
36	"method=ping" during handshake (PING) but the library-bridge ignored
37	that parameter. We now omit this parameter, i.e.
38	LibraryBridgeHelper::PING was removed. Again, this makes things
39	consistent with xdbc-bridge.
40	
41	Side note 2: xdbc-bridge is unchanged in this commit. Therefore,
42	XDBCBridgeHelper now uses the HTTP paths previously in the base class.
43	For funny reason, XDBCBridgeHelper did not use
44	IBridgeHelper::getMainURI() - it generates the URLs by itself. I kept it
45	that way for now but provided an implementation of getMainURI() anyways.
